7.2  Namespaces: a private view of the system

TL;DR
  - A namespace gives a process its own copy of one global thing: process IDs
    (pid), mounts (mnt), network (net), hostname (uts), IPC, users, cgroups,
    clocks (time).
  - A container is mostly a process with new namespaces of every kind: it sees
    only its own processes, files and network.
  - Each process's namespaces are links in /proc/PID/ns/; two processes share
    one when the numbers match.

      lsns                                   # namespaces and their processes
      sudo unshare --pid --fork --mount-proc bash   # then ps: bash is PID 1
      sudo nsenter -t PID -n ip addr         # run a command in PID's network

On your machine
  Here      no container or virtual machine detected
  Isolated  none: the host's namespaces (pid, user, uts, ipc, cgroup, time)
  Can run   VMs with KVM (/dev/kvm) · containers with Docker
  Overlays  1 overlay mount: container layers, usually

Pitfalls
  - A process in a PID namespace has two PIDs: its own inside, and another as
    the host sees it. Use the host's with kill from outside.
  - A new network namespace has only a down loopback interface: containers get a
    virtual cable (veth) to a bridge on the host.
  - Ubuntu 24.04 and later restrict unprivileged user namespaces through
    AppArmor: rootless tools may need a profile there.

Deep dive

  ▾ How do namespaces come about?

    With clone() or unshare() and flags like CLONE_NEWPID or CLONE_NEWNET, a
    process asks the kernel for new namespaces for itself or its children;
    setns() joins existing ones, which is what nsenter and docker exec do. A
    namespace lives as long as a process or a bind mount keeps it.

  ▾ Why are user namespaces special?

    They map user IDs: root (0) inside can be an ordinary user outside. That
    lets an unprivileged user create the other namespaces and run rootless
    containers (7.5), and limits the damage if something escapes. They also
    widened the kernel's attack surface, which is why some distros restrict
    them.

Related
  Commands  lsns, unshare, nsenter
  Paths     /proc/self/ns/
  See also  idk 7.1 What a container really is
  See also  idk 7.5 Root in a container: users, capabilities and the docker
            group
